Looking at this problem, I need to analyze the structure of $v(n)$ and find where $v(n) - v(n-1) < 0$.

## Setup

Let $Q(m)$ = number of square-free positive integers $\leq m$, so $f_n(x) = Q(\lfloor n/x \rfloor)$.

By symmetry in $i,j$:
$$v(n) = 2n\sum_{i=1}^n f_n(i^2) - 6\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n f_n(ij)$$

Define $S_1(n) = \sum_{i: i^2 \leq n} Q(\lfloor n/i^2\rfloor)$ and $S_2(n) = \sum_{ij \leq n} Q(\lfloor n/(ij)\rfloor)$, so $v(n) = 2n\,S_1(n) - 6\,S_2(n)$.

## Key Simplification of $S_1$

$S_1(n) - S_1(n-1) = \sum_{i: i^2|n} \mathbf{1}[n/i^2 \text{ square-free}]$. For $n = \prod p_i^{a_i}$, the valid $i = \prod p_i^{b_i}$ requires $b_i \leq \lfloor a_i/2\rfloor$ and $a_i - 2b_i \leq 1$. If $a_i$ is even, $b_i = a_i/2$ uniquely; if $a_i$ is odd, $b_i = (a_i-1)/2$ uniquely. So **exactly one** valid $i$ exists for every $n$, giving $S_1(n) - S_1(n-1) = 1$, hence $S_1(n) = n$.

## Key Simplification of $\Delta S_2$

$$\Delta S_2(n) = S_2(n)-S_2(n-1) = \sum_{m|n,\; n/m\text{ sf}} d(m) = \sum_{k|\text{rad}(n)} d(n/k)$$

Writing $n = \prod p_i^{a_i}$, each prime factor independently contributes $\sum_{s\in\{0,1\}}(a_i - s + 1) = (a_i+1)+a_i = 2a_i+1$, so:

$$\boxed{\Delta S_2(n) = \prod_{p^a \| n} (2a+1) =: g(n)}$$

## The Difference Formula

$$v(n) - v(n-1) = 4n - 2 - 6\,g(n)$$

We need $g(n) > \frac{2n-1}{3}$.

## Checking all $n \in [2,100]$

| Form | $g(n)$ | Condition | Working examples |
|------|--------|-----------|------------------|
| $p$ | $3$ | $p < 5$ | $2, 3$ |
| $p^2$ | $5$ | $p^2 < 8$ | $4$ |
| $p^3$ | $7$ | $p^3 < 11$ | $8$ |
| $pq$ | $9$ | $pq \leq 13$ | $6, 10$ |
| $p^2q$ | $15$ | $p^2q \leq 22$ | $12, 18, 20$ |
| $p^3q$ | $21$ | $p^3q \leq 31$ | $24$ |
| $pqr$ | $27$ | $pqr \leq 40$ | $30$ |
| $p^2q^2$ | $25$ | $p^2q^2 \leq 37$ | $36$ |
| $p^2qr$ | $45$ | $p^2qr \leq 67$ | $60$ |
| $p^4, p^5, p^4q, p^3q^2, p^5q, p^3qr, pqrs, \ldots$ | various | all fail in range | none |

**Verification of $n=60$:** $60 = 2^2\cdot 3\cdot 5$, $g(60)=5\cdot3\cdot3=45$, $v(60)-v(59)=240-2-270=-32<0$. ✓

**Near-misses confirming 60 is the largest:**
- $n=84=2^2\cdot3\cdot7$: $g=45$, diff $= 336-2-270=+64>0$ ✗
- $n=90=2\cdot3^2\cdot5$: $g=45$, diff $= 360-2-270=+88>0$ ✗
- $n=72=2^3\cdot3^2$: $g=35$, diff $= 288-2-210=+76>0$ ✗
- $n=42=2\cdot3\cdot7$: $g=27$, diff $= 168-2-162=+4>0$ ✗
- $n=48=2^4\cdot3$: $g=27$, diff $= 192-2-162=+28>0$ ✗

All $n \in [61,100]$ give positive differences. The complete list of $n$ with negative differences is $\{2,3,4,6,8,10,12,18,20,24,30,36,60\}$.

## Answer

The largest positive integer $2 \leq n \leq 100$ for which $v(n)-v(n-1)$ is negative is $\boxed{60}$.

